%% Chapter 40: Parser Combinators
\label{ch:parser-combinators}

\section{Introduction to Parser Combinators}
\label{sec:combinators-intro}

Parser combinators provide a \emph{functional} approach to parsing: parsers are first-class values that can be constructed, combined, and passed as arguments to other functions. Rather than generating a parser from a grammar specification (as Yacc or ANTLR do), parser combinators build the parser directly in the host programming language using a library of combinator functions.

The combinator approach is particularly natural in functional programming languages like Haskell, OCaml, and Scala, where higher-order functions and algebraic data types are first-class citizens. However, combinator libraries have been developed for imperative languages as well, including Rust (nom), Python (parsy), and Java (JParsec).

\begin{historical}
The idea of parser combinators dates to the work of Burge (1975) and was further developed by Wadsworth (1985), Hutton (1992), and Fokker (1995). The Parsec library (Leijen and Meijer, 2001) brought parser combinators to a wide audience with its efficient implementation and rich combinator library. Megaparsec (Mark Karpov, 2015) improved on Parsec with better error messages. In the Rust ecosystem, nom (Geoffroy Couprie, 2015) provides a zero-copy parser combinator library. The connection between parser combinators and monads was established by Wadler (1992) and Hutton (1992).
\end{historical}

\section{Functional Approach to Parsing}
\label{sec:functional-parsing}

\subsection{Parsers as Functions}
\label{sec:parsers-as-functions}

In the combinator approach, a parser is simply a function that takes an input string and returns a parse result:

\begin{definition}[Parser Type]
\label{def:parser-type}
A \textbf{parser} is a function of type:
\[
\texttt{Parser } a = \texttt{String} \to [(a, \texttt{String})]
\]
The parser takes an input string and returns a list of possible results, where each result is a pair of the parsed value and the remaining input. The list may be empty (parse failure), contain one element (unambiguous parse), or contain multiple elements (ambiguous parse).
\end{definition}

\begin{example}[Simple Parsers]
\label{ex:simple-parsers}
\begin{verbatim}
  -- Parse a specific character
  char :: Char -> Parser Char
  char c = \input -> case input of
    (x:xs) | x == c -> [(c, xs)]
    _                -> []
  
  -- Parse any digit
  digit :: Parser Char
  digit = satisfy isDigit
  
  -- Parse any character satisfying a predicate
  satisfy :: (Char -> Bool) -> Parser Char
  satisfy p = \input -> case input of
    (x:xs) | p x -> [(x, xs)]
    _            -> []
\end{verbatim}
\end{example}

\section{Combinator Libraries}
\label{sec:combinator-libraries}

\subsection{Basic Combinators}
\label{sec:basic-combinators}

The fundamental combinators are:

\begin{definition}[Choice Combinator]
\label{def:choice}
$\texttt{p <|> q}$ tries parser $\texttt{p}$ first; if it fails, tries parser $\texttt{q}$:
\[
(\texttt{p <|> q})\; s = \texttt{p}\; s \;{+\!+}\; \texttt{q}\; s
\]
(where $++$ is list concatenation, or $\texttt{q}\; s$ is tried only if $\texttt{p}\; s = []$)
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Sequence Combinator (Bind)]
\label{def:bind}
$\texttt{p >>= f}$ applies parser $\texttt{p}$, then applies the function $\texttt{f}$ to the result to obtain a new parser, which is applied to the remaining input:
\[
(\texttt{p >>= f})\; s = [(b, s'') \mid (a, s') \leftarrow \texttt{p}\; s, (b, s'') \leftarrow \texttt{f}\; a\; s']
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Many Combinator]
\label{def:many}
$\texttt{many}\; \texttt{p}$ applies parser $\texttt{p}$ zero or more times, collecting the results in a list:
\begin{verbatim}
  many p = many1 p <|> return []
  many1 p = do { x <- p; xs <- many p; return (x:xs) }
\end{verbatim}
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[SepBy Combinator]
\label{def:sepby}
$\texttt{sepBy}\; p\; sep$ parses zero or more occurrences of $\texttt{p}$, separated by $\texttt{sep}$:
\begin{verbatim}
  sepBy p sep = sepBy1 p sep <|> return []
  sepBy1 p sep = do { x <- p; xs <- many (sep >> p); return (x:xs) }
\end{verbatim}
\end{definition}

\subsection{Combinator Examples}
\label{sec:combinator-examples}

\begin{example}[Parsing Arithmetic Expressions]
\label{ex:combinator-expr}
\begin{verbatim}
  expr   = term `chainl1` addop
  term   = factor `chainl1` mulop
  factor = nat <|> char '(' *> expr <* char ')'
  
  addop  = char '+' *> return (+) <|> char '-' *> return (-)
  mulop  = char '*' *> return (*) <|> char '/' *> return (div)
  
  chainl1 p op = do
    x <- p
    rest x
    where rest x = (do f <- op; y <- p; rest (f x y)) <|> return x
\end{verbatim}
\end{example}

\section{Monadic Parser Combinators}
\label{sec:monadic-combinators}

\subsection{Parsers as Monads}
\label{sec:parsers-monads}

The bind combinator $(>>=)$ and the return combinator make parsers an instance of the \emph{monad} type class. This provides a clean, compositional interface for building parsers.

\begin{definition}[Parser Monad]
\label{def:parser-monad}
The parser monad is defined by:
\begin{verbatim}
  instance Monad Parser where
    return a = \input -> [(a, input)]
    p >>= f  = \input -> concatMap (\(a, rest) -> f a rest) (p input)
\end{verbatim}
\end{definition}

The monadic interface allows parsers to be written in \texttt{do}-notation, which reads like a sequence of parsing steps:

\begin{verbatim}
  ifStmt = do
    string "if"
    whitespace
    cond <- expr
    whitespace
    string "then"
    whitespace
    thenBranch <- stmt
    whitespace
    string "else"
    whitespace
    elseBranch <- stmt
    return (If cond thenBranch elseBranch)
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Applicative Parsing}
\label{sec:applicative}

The \emph{applicative} interface is a simpler alternative to monadic parsing that provides better static analysis and optimization opportunities:

\begin{definition}[Applicative Parser]
\label{def:applicative-parser}
\begin{verbatim}
  instance Functor Parser where
    fmap f p = fmap (\(a, s) -> (f a, s)) p
  
  instance Applicative Parser where
    pure a = \input -> [(a, input)]
    pf <*> px = \input -> [(f x, s'') | (f, s') <- pf input, (x, s'') <- px s']
\end{verbatim}
\end{definition}

Applicative parsers can be analyzed statically (e.g., to determine the set of possible first tokens) because the structure of the parser is known in advance.

\subsection{MonadPlus and Alternative}
\label{sec:monadplus}

\begin{definition}[MonadPlus Parser]
\label{def:monadplus-parser}
\begin{verbatim}
  instance MonadPlus Parser where
    mzero = \input -> []
    p `mplus` q = \input -> case p input of
      [] -> q input
      rs -> rs
\end{verbatim}
\end{definition}

The \texttt{mplus} combinator implements \emph{committed choice}: if the first parser succeeds, the second is not tried. This is essential for efficient parsing.

\section{Packrat Parsing and Memoization}
\label{sec:packrat}

\subsection{The Problem with Backtracking}
\label{sec:backtracking-problem}

Simple parser combinators with backtracking can have exponential worst-case time complexity. For example, the parser:

\begin{verbatim}
  p = (string "aaa" <|> string "aab") >> string "ac"
\end{verbatim}

on the input \texttt{aaac} will try both alternatives for the first part, each consuming two characters and then failing.

\subsection{Packrat Parsing}
\label{sec:packrat-parsing}

\emph{Packrat parsing} (Ford, 2002) solves this problem by \emph{memoizing} the results of all parser calls:

\begin{definition}[Packrat Parser]
\label{def:packrat}
A \textbf{packrat parser} memoizes the result of every parser at every input position. Before parsing, the parser checks if the result for the current position is already in the memo table. If so, it returns the cached result. Otherwise, it parses normally and stores the result in the memo table.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Packrat Complexity]
\label{thm:packrat-complexity}
Packrat parsing runs in $O(n)$ time and $O(n)$ space for any parsing expression grammar (PEG), where $n$ is the length of the input.
\end{theorem}

\subsection{Implementation of Packrat Parsing}
\label{sec:packrat-impl}

Packrat parsing is implemented by wrapping each parser with a memoization layer:

\begin{verbatim}
  memo :: Parser a -> Parser a
  memo p = \input pos ->
    case lookup (p, pos) memoTable of
      Just result -> result
      Nothing     -> let result = p input pos
                     in  memoTable[pos := result]
                         result
\end{verbatim}

The memo table is a hash map from (parser identity, input position) to parse result.

\subsection{Limitations of Packrat Parsing}
\label{sec:packrat-limits}

Packrat parsing has several limitations:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Memory usage}: The memo table requires $O(n \cdot |G|)$ space, where $|G|$ is the size of the grammar. For large inputs, this can be significant.
  \item \textbf{Left recursion}: Packrat parsers (like PEGs in general) do not handle left recursion directly. Special extensions are required.
  \item \textbf{No ambiguity}: Packrat parsing always produces a single parse tree (the first successful alternative).
\end{itemize}

\section{Comparison with Generated Parsers}
\label{sec:combinator-vs-generated}

\subsection{Advantages of Combinators}
\label{sec:combinator-advantages}

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Integration with host language}: Parsers are written in the same language as the rest of the compiler, with full access to the type system, libraries, and debugging tools.
  \item \textbf{Composability}: Parsers are first-class values that can be combined, parameterized, and abstracted over.
  \item \textbf{Type safety}: The type system ensures that parsers are well-formed and that parse results have the correct types.
  \item \textbf{No separate tool}: No need for a separate parser generator tool or build step.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Advantages of Generated Parsers}
\label{sec:generated-advantages}

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Performance}: Generated parsers (especially LR) are typically faster than combinator parsers.
  \item \textbf{Error recovery}: Generated parsers have well-developed error recovery mechanisms.
  \item \textbf{Grammar analysis}: Generated parsers analyze the grammar for conflicts and ambiguities at generation time.
  \item \textbf{Familiarity}: Yacc/Bison and ANTLR are widely known and used.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{When to Use Each}
\label{sec:when-to-use}

