\section{Implementations}
\label{sec:combinator-implementations}

\subsection{Parsec}
\label{sec:parsec}

Parsec (Leijen and Meijer, 2001) is the most widely used parser combinator library for Haskell. It provides:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Monadic and applicative interfaces
  \item Committed choice (no backtracking after a committed parse)
  \item Descriptive error messages with source positions
  \item A rich library of combinators
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Megaparsec}
\label{sec:megaparsec}

Megaparsec (Karpov, 2015) is a modern replacement for Parsec with:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Better error messages (including hints and expected tokens)
  \item Better performance
  \item More flexible token streams (not just strings)
  \item Improved API design
\end{itemize}

\subsection{nom (Rust)}
\label{sec:nom}

\texttt{nom} (Couprie, 2015) is a parser combinator library for Rust that emphasizes:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Zero-copy parsing (parsers operate on byte slices without copying)
  \item Streaming support (incremental parsing of data arriving in chunks)
  \item High performance through inlining and specialization
\end{itemize}

\begin{verbatim}
  fn parse_number(input: &[u8]) -> IResult<&[u8], u32> {
    map_res(
      take_while1(|c: u8| c.is_ascii_digit()),
      |s: &[u8]| std::str::from_utf8(s).unwrap().parse::<u32>()
    )(input)
  }
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Other Implementations}
\label{sec:other-combinators}

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{parboiled2} (Scala): Type-safe parser combinators with compile-time grammar checking
  \item \textbf{parsy} (Python): Simple parser combinator library for Python
  \item \textbf{Parsimonious} (Python): PEG-based parser using combinators
  \item \textbf{LanguageExt} (C\#): Parser combinators for C\#
  \item \textbf{chumsky} (Rust): Modern parser combinator library with good error recovery
\end{itemize}

\section{Summary}
\label{sec:combinator-summary}

Parser combinators provide an elegant, functional approach to parsing that integrates naturally with the host programming language. Key concepts include:

\begin{itemize}
  \item Parsers as first-class functions that can be combined and composed
  \item Monadic and applicative interfaces for clean, compositional parser construction
  \item Packrat parsing for guaranteed linear-time performance
  \item Rich combinator libraries for common parsing patterns
  \item Trade-offs between combinators (flexibility, type safety) and generated parsers (performance, error recovery)
\end{itemize}

\begin{historical}
Parser combinators have evolved from a theoretical curiosity to a practical tool used in production systems. Key milestones include: Burge's early work (1975); Wadsworth's combinator framework (1985); Hutton's monadic parsers (1992); Wadler's ``How to Replace Failure by a List of Successes'' (1985); Leijen and Meijer's Parsec (2001); Ford's packrat parsing and PEGs (2002, 2004); Karpov's Megaparsec (2015); Couprie's nom (2015); and the ongoing development of modern combinator libraries with better error messages, streaming support, and performance.
\end{historical}

