\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\begin{tikzpicture}[tome diagram,x=1.6cm,y=1cm]
  \foreach \i in {0,...,4}{
    \node at (\i,0.6) {\i};
  }
  \foreach \i/\upper/\lower in {0/{[}/{[},1/7/7,2/{[}/{[},3/9/{;},4/{;}/{;}}{
    \node[mem cell,minimum width=1.55cm] at (\i,0) {\upper};
    \node[mem cell,minimum width=1.55cm] at (\i,-1.6) {\lower};
  }
  \draw[tome arrow] (0,-0.45) -- node[below] {item 7} (2,-0.45);
  \draw[tome arrow] (2,-0.45) -- node[below] {item 9} (4,-0.45);
  \draw[tome arrow] (0,-2.08) -- node[below] {item 7} (2,-2.08);
  \draw[tome arrow] (2,-2.08) -- (3,-2.08);
  \node[anchor=west] at (3,-2.42) {number required};
  \node[anchor=west,align=left] at (4.63,0) {stop at 4\\leave \texttt{;}};
  \node[anchor=west,align=left] at (4.63,-1.6) {committed\\failure at 3};
\end{tikzpicture}
\caption{Two runs of \texttt{many} on an item consisting of
\texttt{[} followed by a number. In the upper run the next attempt
fails immediately at index 4, so the separator remains unread. In the
lower run the second attempt has already consumed its opening bracket
when it fails, so the repetition propagates the error.}
\label{fig:visual-40}
\end{figure}

At the loop head, $v$ covers exactly the successful attempts before $i$;
the failed attempt is never added. With finite input, each success moves
at least one token, so there are at most as many successes as remaining
tokens. This bounds calls to $p$, not the internal cost of each call.
An explicit backtracking wrapper changes what counts as a committed failure;
adding one around the entire item is therefore a grammar decision, not
a performance tweak.

\section{Advanced Combinator Patterns}
\label{sec:advanced-patterns}

\subsection{Indentation-Sensitive Parsing}
\label{sec:indentation-parsing}

Languages like Python and Haskell use indentation to delimit blocks. Parser combinators can handle this with a custom \emph{layout} combinator:

\begin{definition}[Layout Combinator]
\label{def:layout}
A \textbf{layout combinator} $\texttt{layout}\; p$ parses a sequence of $p$-results that are indented more than the current indentation level:
\begin{verbatim}
  layout p = do
    indent <- currentIndent
    items <- many (do
      checkIndent (> indent)
      item <- p
      return item)
    return items
\end{verbatim}
\end{definition}

\subsection{Operator Parsing with Precedence}
\label{sec:operator-parsing}

The \texttt{chainl1} and \texttt{makeExprParser} combinators handle operator precedence:

\begin{definition}[Expression Parser]
\label{def:expr-parser}
\begin{verbatim}
  makeExprParser :: Parser a -> [[Operator a]] -> Parser a
  data Operator a = InfixN (Parser (a -> a -> a))  -- non-assoc
                  | InfixL (Parser (a -> a -> a))  -- left-assoc
                  | InfixR (Parser (a -> a -> a))  -- right-assoc
                  | Prefix (Parser (a -> a))
                  | Postfix (Parser (a -> a))
\end{verbatim}
The table of operators is organized by precedence level (highest first).
\end{definition}

\begin{example}[Expression Parser with Precedence]
\label{ex:expr-parser}
\begin{verbatim}
  exprParser = makeExprParser term
    [ [ InfixL (char '*' *> return (*))
      , InfixL (char '/' *> return (div)) ]
    , [ InfixL (char '+' *> return (+))
      , InfixL (char '-' *> return (-)) ]
    , [ Prefix (char '-' *> return negate) ]
    ]
  term = char '(' *> exprParser <* char ')' <|> natural
\end{verbatim}
\end{example}
